## 14. Implementation Timeline

### 14.1 Indicative Project Phases

| Phase | Duration | Key Activities |
|---|---|---|
| **1. Full Feasibility Study** | 3–4 months | Site survey, ground-truth demand, detailed engineering, ESIA, willingness-to-pay |
| **2. Permitting & Licensing** | 2–3 months | Distribution licence, land agreements, environmental permit |
| **3. Financing & Procurement** | 3–4 months | Secure grant/debt/equity, EPC tender, supplier selection |
| **4. Construction** | 4–6 months | Civil works, PV installation, battery/inverter, distribution network |
| **5. Commissioning** | 1 month | Testing, metering, connection of first customers |
| **6. Operations** | 25 years | O&M, monitoring, customer management, tariff collection |

**Estimated total pre-operations timeline:** 13–18 months from PFS approval to first customer connection.

### 14.2 Key Milestones

| Milestone | Target |
|---|---|
| PFS completion | {{ generation_date }} |
| Full feasibility study | PFS + 4 months |
| Financial close | PFS + 8 months |
| Construction start | PFS + 10 months |
| First power | PFS + 15 months |
| Full customer connection | PFS + 18 months |

### 14.3 Critical Path Items

1. **Land acquisition / lease agreement** — Must be secured before construction can begin
2. **Distribution licence** — Regulatory approval required for customer connections
3. **Equipment procurement** — Battery and inverter lead times of 2–3 months
4. **Financing** — Grant disbursement schedule drives construction cashflow

### 14.4 Productive Use Development Timeline

Developing productive use of energy (PUE) should run in parallel with infrastructure deployment to ensure demand materialises alongside supply:

| Phase | Timeline | PUE Activities |
|---|---|---|
| **Feasibility** | Months 1–4 | PUE baseline survey; identify value chains for electrification; assess appliance supply chains |
| **Pre-construction** | Months 5–8 | Establish PUE financing facility; procure demonstration equipment; begin business training |
| **Construction** | Months 9–14 | Install PUE anchor equipment; train operators; finalise off-take agreements |
| **Commissioning** | Month 15 | Connect PUE loads first to establish base demand; monitor load profiles |
| **Operations Year 1** | Months 16–27 | Scale PUE programme; track revenue per value chain; adjust tariffs if needed |
| **Operations Year 2+** | Month 28+ | Expand to additional value chains; replicate successful models; evaluate second-wave investments |

See **Section 4b** for the detailed analysis of value chains, business cases, and financing strategies.

### 14.5 Quick Wins

{% if sizing and sizing.pv_capacity_kwp %}
- **Phase 1 connections:** Prioritise anchor loads (hospital, schools, productive-use enterprises) for immediate social and economic impact
- **Productive use support:** Technical assistance for grain processing, cold storage, and light manufacturing to ensure productive demand materialises alongside supply
- **Community engagement:** Begin during feasibility phase to build support, identify PUE champions, and manage expectations
- **Demonstration effect:** Deploy one or two high-visibility PUE appliances (e.g., grain mill, phone charging station) early to catalyse community buy-in
